 "known_gaps_after": [
  "adjacent_attribute is 36/40 (90.0%) against a plain cosine scan's 40/40 -- improved from 23/40 but still BELOW cosine, so USER_MANUAL_DEVELOPER's 'same as plain cosine' claim is still not met on this fixture-free set.",
  "historical_value/home_address is 0/10: the genuine oldest revision ('I live at <a>.') sits 0.18 cosine below the newest ('my home address is now <b>.') when the question says 'home address', and group_relevance_floor (0.06) drops it. Every fix measured for it costs more on the 3-persona chat set than it gains.",
  "historical_value/bank is 4/10 and previous_value/bank 5/6: 'I bank with <Name>.' carries no possessive and no structural class, so it is untagged, and the only rule that would tag it was rejected above.",
  "third_party/inner_possessive is 9/10 and third_party overall 29/30 against a plain scan's 30/30.",
  "The write path costs about 6% more per add_fact (median 19.4 us -> 20.6 us at 10k rows, D=64, durable=none), from the proper-name scan and the wider slot bound. It is a constant, not a growth: tests/test_scale.py's flatness assertion still passes.",
  "Two contacts' copies of one attribute still collide in the other_ namespace when neither is named by a role noun; that is the documented third-party treatment (is_single_valued is False there, so neither supersedes the other).",
  "'Where does <Name> work?' -- a third-person question with no possessive -- still gets a boost of exactly +0.000. Plain cosine answers it correctly (10/10) and the only way to make the layer fire would be a capitalisation-derived entity rule, which this module bans."
 ]
